[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The Location C network is healthy. All four Location C hosts (pc11–pc14, 192.168.200.11–.14) have correct addressing (/24 on eth0) and a working default gateway 192.168.200.1. From pc11, ping to the gateway succeeds (2/2 replies, ~0.65 ms) and its ARP table shows a valid neighbor entry for 192.168.200.1. Likewise, pc12, pc13, and pc14 all successfully ping 192.168.200.1 and see it as REACHABLE in their neighbor tables. Local L2 connectivity is fine (e.g., pc12↔pc11 pings succeed).

Critically, Location C is not isolated from other sites: from pc12, ping to a remote site host (pc4 at 172.16.10.4) succeeds (2/2 replies, ttl=61, ~2 ms), proving router4 (gateway 192.168.200.1) is forwarding traffic upstream. The reachability matrix also shows external hosts can reach Location C (e.g., pc4→pc12 ok). These measurements contradict the reported “router down”/“cut off” symptoms.

Localization: No fault found on router4 (Location C’s gateway) nor on switches 4/5/6; all observed interfaces and forwarding paths operate normally. Mechanism: Successful ARP resolution and ICMP RTTs indicate active L2/L3 forwarding and no packet loss.

Root cause: User misperception or a higher-layer (e.g., DNS/application) issue unrelated to network routing/switching.

Corrective fix: No network device restart required. Advise checking application/DNS settings on affected workstations (e.g., name resolution, proxy, firewall), as IP connectivity to gateway and remote sites is verified working. === END ===